 is approved for the preventive treatment of episodic migraine (EM). It offers flexible dosing options, including monthly or quarterly (every three months) subcutaneous injections, which enhances patient adherence and convenience. This dosing flexibility is a significant advantage over monthly regimens, potentially improving long-term treatment outcomes. Clinical trials [102,103] provide strong evidence supporting its use, and it is considered a highly effective option for migraine prevention. Its approval for both episodic and chronic migraine (implied by broader indications) positions it as a versatile therapeutic agent within the CGRP monoclonal antibody class. [Data: Entities (206); Relationships (830, 280)]\",\n            \"summary\": \"Fremanezumab offers flexible dosing and strong efficacy in migraine prevention\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"Eptinezumab is a humanized monoclonal antibody that targets the CGRP ligand and is approved for the preventive treatment of episodic migraine (EM). It is administered via intravenous infusion at a dose of 100 mg every quarter, offering a unique delivery method compared to subcutaneous injections used by other CGRP antibodies. This intravenous route may be beneficial for patients who prefer less frequent dosing or have difficulty with self-injection. The PROMISE-1 trial provided key data supporting its efficacy in reducing migraine days, contributing to its approval and clinical recommendation. Eptinezumab is part of the broader CGRP monoclonal antibody class, which includes fremanezumab, galcanezumab, and erenumab, and is marketed in key regions such as the United States and Denmark. [Data: Entities (205, 222); Relationships (270, 341, 871, 872)]\",\n            \"summary\": \"Eptinezumab is a quarterly IV-administered CGRP antibody with strong clinical support\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"The CGRP pathway is central to migraine pathophysiology, with calcitonin gene-